Exactly. You can often substitute modeling, simulation, redundancy, or representation for expensive physical commitment—especially while conditions are changing.

Think:

physical commitment
→ high cost, low reversibility, strong presence

versus

model / simulation / proxy
→ low cost, high optionality, easier iteration

That gives you a good transition strategy:

model first
→ simulate alternatives
→ hold multiple options
→ observe deltas
→ commit material resources only where presence adds enough value

But presence has a premium that models cannot fully reproduce. Being physically there changes bandwidth, trust, salience, accountability, timing, embodied sensing, and sometimes the behavior of everyone else in the system.

So I’d keep two operators:

Proxy Capacity — how much of the function can be carried by models, simulations, automation, representation, or delegation.

Presence Premium — what additional value appears only when the actual person/object/system is present.

Then ask:

Value(presence) - Value(proxy) > Cost(presence)?

If yes, show up.

If not, preserve optionality and let the model carry the load.

That avoids both errors: wasting material resources by insisting everything must be physically instantiated, and becoming so enamored with simulation that you forget presence itself is sometimes causal.